<p>We all love to start new things. It’s exciting! The creativity overfloweth! Keeping those things maintained over the longer term is harder work, and can seem boring and routine. It’s an act of resistance to keep caring for the thing you created, but the great thing is that it pays you back more and more the longer that you continue to do it. It’s also work that — in my opinion — you should do yourself, with your own hands and brain. Let me try to explain why.</p><p>I posted at the start of last month about <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/is-this-thing-still-on/">moving this site from Hugo to 11ty</a>, and also moving hosts from Netlify to Bunny.net. You can read the details there if you want, but my point is that it was quite a lot of work. I had to learn how to build stuff with a new static site generator, adjust the previous set up so that it worked with the new system, figure out how to deploy the new pages, change the DNS host (I’m now using <a href="https://dns.kitchen">omg.lol’s own DNS Kitchen, which is exciting!</a>), and so on.</p><p>I did it the old-fashioned way by reading documentation and tutorials, by trying things out and figuring out what the problem was when they went wrong. I also occasionally asked for help from people with expertise when I got really stuck, or to check that I wasn’t about to make a terrible mistake. It all worked, and the result is what you are reading now.</p><p>This blog, in its various incarnations (but with the same content) is nearly 23 years old. I’ve put a lot of time into it cumulatively over those years, writing articles, but also changing the support structures around it as I have done again recently. Why? I make precisely zero income from it (and it costs me small amounts of money). It takes up time that I could use in other ways<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/the-joy-of-maintenance/#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup>. I do it because the very fact of the care that I have put into it, and how <em>substantial</em> it has become through this slow accumulation over time, has made it precious to me, and I want to take care of it.</p><p>The ‘content’ (as I suppose I must call it, but I hate that term) is the key thing, but it needs a safe and welcoming home to live in. It needs a home that I feel can expand or change to accommodate any new direction or focus that this blog might take. I need to feel that I understand the way that the static site generator works so that I don’t feel hesitant to post (because it seems too much of a barrier) or to change something (because I’m afraid to break it). All of that takes a lot of un-glamorous maintenance work.</p><p>As I mentioned, the writing itself is the most important thing, and I am very happy that one of my previous moves (now probably a decade or more ago) involved moving from a database-driven blog to a static site generator, with each article as a separate Markdown file. That single change makes the whole thing much more adaptable. In the very worst case scenario, I always have all my years of writing on my own hard drive (and backed up in multiple places of course) in plain text that I can read with my own human eyeballs. The metadata for each article (like the date of publication, tags and so on) are also in plain text at the top of the file in YAML format (also human-readable), and easy to change with standard search and replace tools. As a belt-and-braces measure, I have also always named each file the same way: <code>year-month-day-title-of-article.md</code>. That means that I can use standard Unix tools like <code>find</code> and <code>grep</code> (or their modern Rust equivalents) to look for a title that I half remember, or find all the posts from a particular month and year, all on my own computer. The YAML front matter is also great for progressively enhancing posts by adding features. Most static site generators will just ignore a bit of metadata it doesn’t know what to do with, or which you tell it to ignore if missing. So, for example, I have added a <code>mastoURL</code> entry to my frontmatter fairly recently, to which I add the URL of the post I make on Mastodon announcing each new article. That then pulls in information for the purple Mastodon ‘card’ you see at the bottom of the post. However, I have set up a bit of code in the template for each post that checks if that metadata is there, and only tries to include the card if so. This meant that I did not have to go back to thousands of old posts and add even an empty frontmatter entry for <code>mastoURL</code>: older posts don’t have it, and the template quietly skips doing anything about it.</p><p>Other things do break in the content, or at least are not presented consistently (like images or code snippets). For those, I have to do a search to find the older syntax, and then I update it manually to be consistent with the newer system. I do that by hand, bit by bit. I have a hundred or so more pages to clean up, which I am making gradual progress on. I’m actually future-proofing it more, because the images will be represented by plain Markdown image format in the text, which then gets translated by a clever plugin (<a href="https://github.com/Antonio-Laguna/markdown-it-image-figures">markdown-it-image-figures</a>) into proper <code>&lt;figure&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;</code> HTML markup, with the title element placed in a caption element, and the description made into alt text. I have learned a lot more about accessibility over the years, so I am determined to add proper human-written alt text to all the images eventually.</p><p>“But bsag,” I hear some of you asking, “you do know that you can ask an AI agent to do all that for you, don’t you? It would save you so much time and effort.”</p><p>First, nope. Just no. I have massive issues with the ethical, environmental, societal, and cognitive impacts of AI and so I don’t ever knowingly use it, for anything.</p><p>Second, for the alt text, I put that image in there for a reason, so <em>I</em> need to be the one provide a description that encapsulates that for readers who can’t see the image.</p><p>Third, I would miss a brilliant opportunity to go back and re-read pieces I wrote some time ago. When you write continually for more than 20 years, you forget a lot of it, so re-reading it re-surfaces memories, helping you connect how you felt then to how you feel now. Sometimes I’m pleasantly impressed by what I wrote in the past, sometimes there are things that make me cringe a bit (or a lot), but I think it’s all part of me: sometimes who I still am, and sometimes who I used to be. Having a reason to re-read or re-visit random bits again is an important part of reminding me of the value (to me, at least) of the whole tottering pile of stuff here, and therefore remind me why it is important for me to care for and tend it.</p><p>I totally understand people who would rather delete old material every now and then, and start afresh. It’s your site, of course, and you are free to do whatever you like with it. However, I get a lot of joy and reassurance from caring for all of this, making sure that it continues to be accessible and available, reminding me of my strengths, successes, flaws and mistakes. In other words, reminding me that I am a totally ordinary, messy, wonderful human.</p><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Goodness knows, I have enough other hobbies to be getting on with. <a href="https://www.rousette.org.uk/archives/the-joy-of-maintenance/#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>